$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>What the Protection Officer actually does</h2>
<p>Section 9 of the Act assigns the Protection Officer a list of duties that together amount to case-building and welfare logistics: assisting the Magistrate; preparing and forwarding the DIR to the Magistrate, the police and service providers; making applications for protection orders on the aggrieved person&rsquo;s request; ensuring legal aid through the Legal Services Authority; maintaining lists of service providers, shelter homes and medical facilities; arranging shelter and medical examination where needed; and ensuring that monetary relief orders are complied with. Under Section 30, Protection Officers are public servants; under Section 33, dereliction of their statutory duties without sufficient cause is itself punishable — a reminder that the office carries obligations, not just powers.</p>

<h2>The DIR, deconstructed</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Complaint received.</strong> The aggrieved person approaches the Protection Officer or a registered service provider — directly, through the police, or via a medical facility.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>DIR prepared.</strong> In the prescribed form: particulars of the aggrieved person and respondents, the nature of the domestic relationship, dated incidents of physical, sexual, verbal-emotional and economic abuse, documents collected, and the reliefs the complainant seeks.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Forwarded.</strong> Copies go to the Magistrate within whose jurisdiction the application will lie, and to the police station and service providers concerned.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Considered.</strong> When an application under Section 12 is filed, the Magistrate considers the DIR before passing orders — including interim and ex parte orders under Section 23.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why the DIR matters to both sides</h2>
<p>For the applicant, the DIR is an early, contemporaneous record. Interim relief under Section 23 is often decided on the application and the DIR alone, so an incident narrative that is specific — dates, places, injuries, witnesses, documents — converts directly into interim protection. Vague DIRs produce vague orders. For the respondent, the DIR fixes the complainant&rsquo;s first version. Where the Section 12 application, the affidavit and the eventual testimony enlarge or contradict that version, the sequence of documents tells the story, and cross-examination begins with the DIR. Respondents should obtain it at the first hearing and map every later pleading against it.</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Stage</th><th>DIR&rsquo;s role</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Filing (S.12)</td><td>Accompanies or precedes the application; Magistrate considers it before orders</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Interim relief (S.23)</td><td>Primary factual input for ex parte and interim protection, residence and monetary orders</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Trial</td><td>Contemporaneous record against which later versions are tested</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Breach proceedings (S.31)</td><td>Part of the background record establishing the protection order&rsquo;s factual basis</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practical guidance</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>For complainants:</strong> treat the DIR interview seriously — carry documents, name witnesses, give dates. Reliefs not flagged in the DIR can still be claimed, but consistency from the first document strengthens every later stage.</p>
  <p><strong>For respondents:</strong> seek the DIR immediately, verify whether one was prepared at all, and note who prepared it — Protection Officer or service provider — and when, relative to the application.</p>
  <p><strong>For both:</strong> remember the Protection Officer answers to the Magistrate. Grievances about inaction or overreach belong in an application to the court, not in confrontation with the officer.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer describes the statutory machinery in general terms and is not advice on any particular matter. Local practice directions in Delhi&rsquo;s districts refine how DIRs are called for and used.</div>
